    {
      "id": "claim_ef49af581bf24235aaed9bb18894fa42",
      "statement": "IPC challenge-response authentication uses the dedicated `ipc_auth_key` subkey (BLAKE2b domain-separated with context `oifs.kdf.v2.ipc.auth.blake2b`) for mutual authentication. The protocol exchanges 32-byte nonces, computes BLAKE2b-MAC tags over a transcript including direction byte, both nonces, and the filesystem salt. For encrypted images, the client derives the key from the password + salt; for unencrypted images, the client uses a 32-byte session secret published in the `.image.master` rendezvous file. The password never travels on the wire. Direction byte (0/1) in the transcript prevents tag reflection. A 10-second handshake timeout prevents Slowloris attacks. Authenticated streams are pooled to avoid repeated handshakes.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://src/encryption.rs#L123-L124",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c0bb10056f225c4731226f2f3336d34483b9dde2c6d6595f3956ce29e1d67307:"
